  "notes": [
    "Regular reflexive -AR verb — follows standard -AR endings with no exceptions",
    "Affirmative imperative attaches the reflexive pronoun with a written accent to preserve stress: *mójate, mójese, mojémonos, mójense*; negative imperative keeps the pronoun separate: *no te mojes*...",
    "Past participle: *mojado* (regular; also a common adjective meaning \"wet\")",
    "Means \"to get wet\" — *me mojé los pies en el río* (I got my feet wet in the river), *no te mojes con la lluvia* (don't get wet in the rain)",
    "Also used figuratively (especially in Spain) to mean \"to commit oneself\" or \"to take a stand\" on a controversial issue: *el político no quiso mojarse sobre el tema* (the politician didn't want to commit to a position on the issue)",
    "Base verb *mojar* (non-reflexive, \"to wet/dampen\" something else) is the transitive counterpart: *mojar el pan en la salsa* (to dip the bread in the sauce)",
    "Related: *mojado/a* (wet), *mojadura* (soaking, drenching)"
  ],
